xmodel/sol-round5.log:12654:SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md:1133:replay says 5,348 -- 4 terms (0.07%) from the max-pivot echelon:
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12702:+# Instrument Round 5: from a D23 fiber to a germ test and D25
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12710:+5. **DESIGN:** emit only pristine Row 24, Schur its rank-4 ten-variable frontier to at most five residuals, reduce their base coefficients through the cached 509-element (G_{23}), and build the other-35-fiber D23 atlas before launching family-wide D25 solves.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12914:+### 2.3 Does it predict D25?
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12916:+**FACT:** not theoremically. Row 24 has nine η components and a rank-4 ten-variable first-occurrence symbol, hence at most five existential compatibility residuals. For a fixed full D23 point, only the six new level-56 variables move, their sub-symbol has rank two, and seven residuals can remain. Neither count inherits the D23 height-two result.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12918:+**CONJECTURE K-D25:** if (K) is a genuine depth-ladder component rather than an accidental embedded component, the five Row-24 residuals should again have conormal rank well below five after reduction on each 11-dimensional survivor component. The first discriminating calculation is the generic conormal rank of the reduced Row-24 residuals at the six witnesses and then modulo each minimal component of (G_{23}). Rank 1–2 predicts a 9–10-dimensional D25 projection; rank 5 predicts roughly dimension 6, still not generic emptiness. A D25 kill would require an exceptional nonlinear incompatibility or unit ideal, not merely “five equations.”
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12920:+## 3. D25 quotient/Schur compiler and the family lift
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12934:+so the fully registered locus is (\mathbb A^{41}) over the det23 base, dimension 52. Row 24 definitely reactivates the four level-51 coordinates. The compiler input must consequently be
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12945:+1. **Build only pristine Row 24.** Use `VDEG_CAP=25`, B frozen, and `PIN42`, retaining canonical raw names. Assert Row 23 is identically zero and the nonzero η powers are exactly (2,5,\ldots,26). Reproduce the 90-entry first-occurrence digest
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12962:+5. **Emit control and quotient from one source.** The rank-complete control is the full D23 fiber plus all nine pristine Row-24 components: 81 equations and between 92 variables (the guaranteed existing 82 plus the ten frontier coordinates) and the 101-variable full-registry ceiling. The sparse quotient target is the 26-row, 5,348-term D21 fiber core plus (g_1,g_2,g_3), totaling 29 rows and 5,360 terms, followed by at most five reduced D25 residuals. Best case is 22 variables/34 equations; the honest result is ((22+r)) variables/34 equations, with (r) measured. A rank-complete pre-Schur quotient is ((32+r)) variables/38 equations.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12964:+Use the 509-element basis as a normal-form engine; do not prepend its 405,524 terms to an ordinary F4 solve. Accept a quotient point only after reconstructing every eliminated and dormant coordinate and checking every pristine D23 and Row-24 row.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12966:+Required gates are exact source/GB/order/fiber hashes; affine, rank, and chart-unit certificates; absence of removed variables; raw-versus-quotient random evaluation; backsolve at all six witnesses; replay of every pristine Row-24 row; τ and all (h)-sign branches; (W)-chart saturation; the `+42` mutation; cross-prime support; and every rank boundary.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12972:+**ESTIMATE:** pristine Row 24 will contain roughly 40,000–200,000 terms; the full control should be about 100,000–260,000 terms or 2–10 MB. The existing nonincremental Python builder may take 1–4 hours; a slot-incremental cached series builder should take 5–30 minutes. Once the raw row exists, Schur plus coefficient normal forms should take seconds to a few minutes and stay below 1 GB if the reduced block stays under 100,000 terms. Warn at 100,000 terms or degree 16; stop expansion at 250,000 terms and retain the hybrid/DAG representation.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12978:+The raw Row-24 generator, dormant registry, rank-4 polewise factorization, Schur code, reconstruction topology, and normal-form compiler are reusable across the (3\cdot3\cdot2\cdot2=36) finite radical components. A specialized D23 ideal/basis is not: identical counts or leading-monomial profiles do not prove an isomorphism between fibers. Cross-prime-identical staircases permit reuse of an operation skeleton only with replayed coefficient-cancellation and support guards.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12982:+> **Target at (p=105337):** every one of the 36 finite radical components has a proper D23 projection of dimension 11/height two, the same initial staircase and conormal-dependency signature, and the uniform rank-4 Row-24 frontier.
xmodel/sol-round5.log:12984:+Merely proving the union nonempty adds nothing to the promoted one-fiber result. Build a 36-fiber D23 normal-form/GB atlas first, hash the feature matrices, and search for an explicit transport between fibers. Baselines are about 11 seconds for a D21 fiber and 63 seconds for representative det23; if they persist, the other 35 components cost about 43 minutes serial per prime and about 0.53 GB of bases per prime (about 2.2 CPU-hours and 1.6 GB for all 36 at three primes). Repeat at (105673) only after the first atlas is understood. Then specialize the five Row-24 residuals over the atlas and compare (r), support, degree, and conormal rank; do not launch 36 D25 F4 jobs before that comparison.
